# Citation metadata.
#
# GitHub renders a "Cite this repository" control from this file. The format
# is Citation File Format 1.2.0; the schema is at https://citation-file-format.github.io/
#
# The phrase itself should be cited to Kernighan rather than to this
# repository; see the references section below and README section 17.

cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If this repository has contributed to your work, please cite it as below.
  If you are citing the phrase rather than this treatment of it, cite
  Kernighan 1972 or Kernighan and Ritchie 1978 in the references section.
title: >-
  Hello, World: An Inquiry into the Descent of a Sentence, the Machine That
  Utters It, and the Question of Whether Anything Is Meant By It
abstract: >-
  A complete C program that prints "Hello, World!" to standard output and
  exits, accompanied by a three-volume treatment of the sentence's descent
  from Proto-Indo-European, the history of the language that utters it, and
  the philosophical status of the utterance. 2,712 lines of documentation
  supporting 6 lines of executable code.
type: software
license: Unlicense
keywords:
  - c
  - hello-world
  - historical-linguistics
  - philosophy-of-language
  - programming-language-history

references:
  - type: report
    title: A Tutorial Introduction to the Language B
    authors:
      - family-names: Kernighan
        given-names: Brian W.
    institution:
      name: Bell Laboratories
    year: 1972
    notes: >-
      Earliest known appearance of the phrase "hello, world".

  - type: book
    title: The C Programming Language
    authors:
      - family-names: Kernighan
        given-names: Brian W.
      - family-names: Ritchie
        given-names: Dennis M.
    publisher:
      name: Prentice-Hall
    year: 1978
    edition: First
    notes: >-
      Fixed the phrase permanently in the culture. 228 pages.
